WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.160
[man yells]

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:08.540
Not gonna happen.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:11.740
Not in the foggiest is
that ever gonna happen.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:14.060
I'm Dwayne Fields, a polar explorer.

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:17.620
I've spent so many years
traveling in and doing work

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:19.740
and carrying out expeditions
in the arctic regions.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:21.580
I'm the first Black man of Britain

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:23.160
to have walked to the magnetic north pole.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:24.900
Today, we're going to be focusing on

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:26.460
polar-exploration movies,

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:29.080
and we're going to be judging
how realistic they are.

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:33.380
[Doc panting]

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:35.680
Jerry: Take deep breaths, Doc.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.120
He's saying, "Take a deep breath."

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:42.080
What you want to do is
slow your breathing down,

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:43.520
control your breathing.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:44.920
It stops you from panicking.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:46.600
What you do is you get your arms out,

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:47.480
you kick your legs,

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.660
and you wiggle your body up onto the ice.

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:51.900
Unless he's got an injury
where he can't kick.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:55.000
Jerry: All right, take the rope.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:55.840
Go to Doc.

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:58.980
He seems relatively calm

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:03.020
despite the shock that
he would be feeling.

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:04.720
I've done exercises

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:07.600
where I've thrown myself
into a hole in ice

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.580
just to get you accustomed

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:11.280
to the feeling and the shock.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.440
You get in there, you dip yourself down.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:16.280
[breathes slowly]

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:20.360
Now you can talk to me,

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:23.840
because now I've brought
everything down a slower pace.

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:25.780
Jerry: She's coming to you.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:28.200
You're putting another body,

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:29.740
whether it's a dog's body, human body,

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:31.260
another body in danger.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:33.380
If the dog falls through the
ice, it's going to panic.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:34.900
It might cause the ice to crack more.

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:38.320
He's also 8, maybe 9 meters away.

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:40.700
He could easily throw
the rope that distance

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:42.620
and save risking the dog's life.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:50.500
Some of the best-trained dogs in the world

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:52.420
still only follow one command.

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:55.000
Go left, go right, go straight, go down,

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.260
come back, sit, jump.

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:00.340
In terms of, "Put this over
Doc's head and shoulder,"

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:02.360
that's not realistic. Let's be honest.

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:07.080
[Jerry yelling]
[dogs barking]

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:11.500
If I'd walked across some ice

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:13.780
and someone behind me
fell through the ice,

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:16.300
I'd assume that there's a weakness there.

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:18.140
My first thing is lay down,

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:20.120
spread myself as flat as I can,

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:24.360
get a board, get a piece of branch,

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:25.720
anything to spread my weight

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:27.160
and to assist the rescue.

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:28.640
Throw the rope to the person.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:30.720
I wouldn't go towards them.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:32.820
I'd give it a 6, because,
like most people,

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:34.740
who doesn't want a well-trained dog?

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.520
[ice cracking]

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:42.260
What they're hoping for
is that sample of ice

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:45.140
has trapped bubbles of air,

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:46.120
which gives them an idea

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:48.240
of what the atmosphere
was like at the time.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:50.820
[ice cracking]

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:54.840
You don't know what the
extent of it's going to be.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:56.080
It does crack.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.160
It's a [imitates ice cracking] sound.

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:01.700
[ice cracking]

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:04.540
If a crack starts to form,

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:05.840
you get away from the crack.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:08.420
You raise the alarm, and
you get away from the crack.

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:11.060
What I found was we'd
go to sleep in the tent

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:13.340
on a bit of frozen ocean,

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:15.820
and we'd wake up to small sounds.

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:17.880
And only when it's silent,
there isn't much wind,

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:19.300
can you hear the slight cracking,

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:21.160
and it almost sounds echo-y

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:24.200
as it echoes and resonates
through the ice itself.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.280
Grabs the samples that he's collected.

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:38.700
It looks like four or five of them.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:41.040
Now, these things aren't light.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:45.440
You cannot jump a 12-foot,

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:47.180
10-foot gap

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:51.940
carrying 20, 25 kilos. You can't do it.

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:53.860
[ice cracking]

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:55.460
Jack!

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:04.040
Not gonna happen.

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:05.420
Not in the foggiest.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:08.180
Can't imagine he would have
been sat in the office,

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:10.140
the office tent, and thought,

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:12.120
"Oh, I'll just keep my ice ax on me."

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:13.940
I've kept it in my bag,

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:16.300
to the side or the back of my bag.

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:18.260
Generally, I don't keep it around my waist

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:20.200
unless I'm using it at that point.

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:23.420
If I'm climbing an ice
wall for recreational usage

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:27.720
or when I go for a walk in high altitude

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:30.480
or snowy terrain, I'll carry one.

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:32.980
In case you slip, you can
use it to stop yourself.

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:36.520
I generally don't walk
around with an ice ax on me.

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:39.980
But the ice itself is what's holding you.

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:42.020
As long as it's cold enough,
the ice is hard enough,

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:43.040
it will hold your weight.

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:45.440
They were made of wood
with maybe a steel tip.

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:47.200
Nowadays they're made of aluminium mostly,

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:49.460
so they're super
lightweight, super strong.

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:51.740
You could comfortably support yourself.

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:53.540
And I'd give it a four.

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:56.620
Entertainment value, it's
an entertainment film.

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:02.020
[woman yells]

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:06.320
She's holding something which
is metal with exposed skin.

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:08.700
You don't want to do that in the cold.

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:11.220
What happens is your skin
is always going to be

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:13.900
slightly warmer than the
thing that you're touching.

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:16.980
That thin layer of moisture on your skin

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:19.260
or warmth on your skin will melt the ice

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:22.920
on the metal object just slightly

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:24.260
so you almost get stuck to it.

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:27.160
Normally, what you'd do is
you'd allow it to warm up.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:31.220
You'd warm it up slowly using,

00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:33.460
arguably, people say, no, use hot water;

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:35.840
other people say use warm water.

00:05:36.940 --> 00:05:38.160
Oh, my God.

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:44.140
That looks like advanced frostbite.

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:47.180
If it wasn't frostbite
and it was frostnip,

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.680
you might see some discoloration.

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:53.600
You might feel some numbness,
some stinging maybe.

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:56.360
I had my goggles on, which was perfect.

00:05:56.360 --> 00:05:59.480
I had a bit of material
just covering the goggle,

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:00.980
and it was just rubbing on my nose,

00:06:00.980 --> 00:06:02.780
and where that material got so cold,

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:06.540
the end of my nose turned
darker and peeled off.

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:09.140
The outer layers of skin were dead.

00:06:09.140 --> 00:06:13.760
With frostbite, the cold
causes all the cells to burst.

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:16.680
If the cell wall is burst, that
cell is dead. It's useless.

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:18.200
The skin cells, they're
all the way through

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:20.460
to the nerve endings.

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:23.140
Nothing?

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:27.520
I'm going to have to amputate this.

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:29.060
No, no way.

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:30.960
Get them taken off as quickly as possible.

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:32.620
Prevent further damage.

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:34.240
It really does get like that.

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:35.640
They start to change color.

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:39.920
It goes from maybe a pale
color to a dark blue.

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:41.360
Then it starts to get darker and darker

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:42.880
until it turns black.

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:44.620
Now, the way you can avoid it is

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:48.120
we layer up, as many layers as you can.

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:50.620
Give that scene a solid 8, 9 out of 10.

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:53.800
[polar bear growling]

00:06:58.940 --> 00:07:00.980
Oh, I like that.

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:03.920
He's taken shelter in
this natural formation,

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:05.140
a cave of some sort.

00:07:05.140 --> 00:07:06.860
And it looks like he's piled up the snow

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:09.780
at the entrance to keep
the air inside warm,

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:12.460
which is a good idea, but
you should be careful,

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:16.880
'cause you don't want the
carbon dioxide to build up.

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:19.880
[polar bear growls]

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:26.660
Polar bears are brilliant hunters.

00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:27.660
They've got one of the best

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:28.960
senses of smell in the natural kingdom.

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:32.460
And you can hear it outside
taking big breaths in.

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:33.980
And that's a telltale sign.

00:07:33.980 --> 00:07:35.600
It's walking around, sniffing the air,

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:37.280
tracking down where that
smell is coming from.

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:40.000
[man yells]

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:42.700
[polar bear growls]

00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:43.880
[man yells]

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:46.260
[polar bear growls]

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:48.400
Remember, these things
are used to hunting seal

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:52.960
and finding carcasses washed
up on beaches from miles away.

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:55.200
Normally, they'd find a
seal underneath the ice.

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:58.620
They'll dig it out, take
the young, take the seal,

00:07:58.620 --> 00:07:59.740
and that's their meal.

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:02.200
So it's doing exactly what
it would normally do there.

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:04.180
[polar bear growls]

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:05.900
[man yells]

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:07.140
[polar bear growls]

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:08.380
Exactly what you're supposed to do.

00:08:08.380 --> 00:08:09.400
Loads of noise,

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:11.560
be ready to defend
yourself if you have to,

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:13.660
and use the flare.

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:16.860
People do get attacked by
polar bears. It happens.

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:19.260
They tend to happen near a settlement,

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:21.960
or it tends to happen when the polar bear

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:24.760
is either ill, finds it difficult to hunt.

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:27.400
I'll give this one a solid 9 out of 10.

00:08:30.620 --> 00:08:32.680
No, thank you.

00:08:33.820 --> 00:08:36.000
I like these better.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:39.400
You can see some frost
starting to form on his jacket.

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:41.020
And I think I can see a little bit forming

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:44.220
on his mustache as well,
so that's pretty accurate.

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:48.360
You don't want to stop for a main meal

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:50.380
every two or three hours.

00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:53.880
So what you do is you
have a bag with food,

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:55.940
the calorific content is very high,

00:08:55.940 --> 00:08:57.420
and you put your hand in your pocket,

00:08:57.420 --> 00:08:59.720
chop that in your mouth every so often.

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:02.260
Now, inevitably you're
going to get someone

00:09:02.260 --> 00:09:04.040
who eats a lot more than you.

00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:07.520
And part of a team dynamic is you stop

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:09.440
and you share out whatever you've got.

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:10.740
Now, if everyone does that,

00:09:10.740 --> 00:09:12.860
it balances out over the
day or over the week.

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:14.340
Look at me.

00:09:15.460 --> 00:09:16.940
Pizza.

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:20.920
Cheeseburgers.

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:22.580
See, sometimes when I'm out,

00:09:22.580 --> 00:09:24.860
I look forward to having a Jelly Baby

00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:26.760
at the next allocated stop

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:28.880
that me or my team have decided.

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:30.420
Could be any treat.

00:09:30.420 --> 00:09:32.800
Just having something to look forward to,

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:34.680
it helps you take that next step.

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:36.680
So, I think that's spot on.

00:09:36.680 --> 00:09:39.480
When I first went to the Arctic,

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:43.700
I think two, three days before the end,

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:44.760
we'd been walking for about,

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:47.460
22 days was the total journey time.

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:50.200
We'd been walking for about 19 hours,

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:53.320
and I remember thinking,
"Oh, gosh, I'm hungry."

00:09:53.320 --> 00:09:54.720
And as soon as I said that,

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:57.500
my mind started to think
about a chicken biryani

00:09:57.500 --> 00:10:00.620
and a lamb korma that I'd
had three weeks earlier.

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:02.320
So I kept looking at my teammates, saying,

00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:04.760
"Oh, gosh, it's like I
can almost smell it."

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:07.480
You keep going in that direction.

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:10.120
Polar winds, it feels agonizing.

00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:14.240
When you see these images
of people in polar extremes

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:17.920
and around their eyes are blistering up

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:18.940
and their lips are cracked,

00:10:18.940 --> 00:10:20.900
it's the wind for the most
part that's doing that.

00:10:20.900 --> 00:10:23.720
Having one huge jacket is good,

00:10:23.720 --> 00:10:26.460
but the best option is
have multiple layers

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:28.200
to trap multiple layers of air.

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:29.780
[wind whooshing]

00:10:29.780 --> 00:10:32.380
There he doesn't have any goggles on.

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:34.500
Luckily, the little girl, I think it is,

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:35.940
does have goggles on.

00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:40.100
And it must be so scary to think that

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:42.760
you can't open your
eyes to try and navigate

00:10:42.760 --> 00:10:43.960
using whatever tools are available.

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:47.100
[wind whooshing]

00:10:49.640 --> 00:10:53.780
What looks like snow or mist
— or, it looks like mist,

00:10:53.780 --> 00:10:56.040
it's actually snow. It's called spindrift,

00:10:56.040 --> 00:10:59.320
and that's the wind kicking
up a small amount of snow

00:10:59.320 --> 00:11:01.700
and making it swirl and blow past.

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:05.700
Generally, at about 10, 15 kilometers,

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:07.740
it will start picking up bits of snow.

00:11:07.740 --> 00:11:10.400
So you can almost tell
roughly what the wind speed is

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:13.480
by how much snow is being been moved along

00:11:13.480 --> 00:11:16.080
and how high up that is.

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:18.180
The details such as the frost,

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:19.980
I'd say I'd give it a solid 8,

00:11:19.980 --> 00:11:21.520
maybe 8 1/2 out of 10.

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:23.620
Man: Observe my signals.

00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:25.920
One pull on the tube
means half a fathom slack.

00:11:27.880 --> 00:11:29.180
Ready!

00:11:30.340 --> 00:11:32.180
It just instantly brought me back to

00:11:32.180 --> 00:11:34.720
the books I've read about
Shackleton and Scott

00:11:34.720 --> 00:11:38.000
and ships being stuck in
Antarctica over winter

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:39.860
because they'd become trapped in the ice.

00:11:39.860 --> 00:11:42.680
And then burning bits of
the ship just to keep warm.

00:11:43.660 --> 00:11:45.260
There should be a surgeon here.

00:11:45.260 --> 00:11:47.520
They're just below, Mr. Collins.

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:49.980
Cold water, your body loses temperature

00:11:49.980 --> 00:11:51.860
about 20 to 25 times quicker.

00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:57.500
He's using what looks like a spear

00:11:57.500 --> 00:11:59.280
to chisel away at the ice.

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:02.780
At this time in history, there weren't any

00:12:02.780 --> 00:12:05.300
tried-and-tested methods
for stuff like this.

00:12:05.300 --> 00:12:07.020
It was, you did what you had to do

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:08.660
using the tools that you had to hand.

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:10.220
I've read accounts of people

00:12:10.220 --> 00:12:12.380
chopping away at the ice using a pickax,

00:12:12.380 --> 00:12:15.220
tying ropes to ships and
pulling them backwards

00:12:15.220 --> 00:12:16.960
and then pulling them forwards again,

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:18.620
and taking a different route

00:12:18.620 --> 00:12:20.580
and using the dogs to pull

00:12:20.580 --> 00:12:22.620
and going ahead and breaking up the ice

00:12:22.620 --> 00:12:23.440
ahead of the ship.

00:12:23.440 --> 00:12:26.160
With modern ships, they've
got hardened hulls.

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:28.180
So the hulls may be twice as thick.

00:12:28.180 --> 00:12:29.820
It's made of steel.

00:12:29.820 --> 00:12:31.600
It's ready for impacting ice.

00:12:31.600 --> 00:12:34.720
They've got powerful engines
that can break the ice up.

00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:37.300
They've got radar to detect
the thickness of the ice.

00:12:44.060 --> 00:12:46.500
I think the Arctic has
this mysticism about it,

00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:48.360
where — we like familiarity.

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:49.560
The thing with Arctic is,

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:52.160
it changes every single year.

00:12:52.160 --> 00:12:54.200
The maps that we have this year,

00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:57.460
they won't be perfect,
because come next year,

00:12:57.460 --> 00:12:59.520
the ice has fallen, some has melted.

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:02.840
Because so much of it isn't visible,

00:13:02.840 --> 00:13:04.960
there's a lot that we don't know,

00:13:04.960 --> 00:13:07.420
and that doesn't sit well

00:13:07.420 --> 00:13:09.120
within the human mind, does it?

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:11.320
Our imaginations start
playing games with us.

00:13:11.320 --> 00:13:13.860
I love this. I'd give
this a solid 9 out of 10.

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:16.980
MacReady: We're not
getting out of here alive.

00:13:18.680 --> 00:13:19.820
But neither is that thing.

00:13:19.820 --> 00:13:20.720
Why would you say that?

00:13:20.720 --> 00:13:23.300
You're perfectly capable,
at least three of you,

00:13:23.300 --> 00:13:24.820
perfectly capable.

00:13:24.820 --> 00:13:26.560
You've got all the tools to escape.

00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:28.400
You've got all the means to make fire

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:30.200
and to get a good distance away.

00:13:30.200 --> 00:13:33.960
If something's happened and
you're isolated, you leave.

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:36.960
You head to the nearest populated area

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:38.040
or a place you could get help

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:39.720
and you radio back what's happened

00:13:39.720 --> 00:13:42.020
or what you need or what
support's necessary.

00:13:48.460 --> 00:13:51.420
I notice that they are lighting

00:13:51.420 --> 00:13:54.140
what looks like TNT?

00:13:54.140 --> 00:13:58.440
Is it trinitrotoluene rods?

00:13:58.440 --> 00:14:00.260
They're lighting it right
next to their chest,

00:14:00.260 --> 00:14:03.160
but they've also got other
rods right next to their chest.

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:05.660
I don't think that's something you'd do.

00:14:05.660 --> 00:14:08.320
[bomb explodes]

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:12.340
Yes, fire is a good option.

00:14:12.340 --> 00:14:14.540
Every single predator
I've ever come across

00:14:14.540 --> 00:14:17.260
is afraid of fire. Simple fact.

00:14:17.260 --> 00:14:19.440
The only things that
are attracted by fire,

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:21.760
generally, are insects.

00:14:21.760 --> 00:14:23.360
I don't think it's ever a good idea

00:14:23.360 --> 00:14:26.740
to blow up a hole in
the middle of your camp.

00:14:26.740 --> 00:14:29.140
You've got what looks like
a tracked vehicle there,

00:14:29.140 --> 00:14:30.540
which is, it could be some kind of

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:32.280
small bulldozer or lifter.

00:14:32.280 --> 00:14:36.220
You've got the means to escape
and to survive short term.

00:14:36.220 --> 00:14:37.540
The biggest threat out there

00:14:37.540 --> 00:14:39.980
is never going to be the wildlife.

00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:41.800
If you prepare for that, it's fine.

00:14:41.800 --> 00:14:43.020
If you carry a shotgun

00:14:43.020 --> 00:14:45.000
for the most extremes of circumstances.

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:47.720
The biggest threat's always
going to be exposure.

00:14:47.720 --> 00:14:49.760
Making sure I'm prepared for whatever

00:14:49.760 --> 00:14:51.140
the weather is going to throw at me.

00:14:51.140 --> 00:14:54.000
No, not likely to face an alien. [laughs]

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:56.060
I love a sci-fi movie more than most,

00:14:56.060 --> 00:14:57.700
so I'm going to give this one a 5,

00:14:57.700 --> 00:14:59.440
but in terms of how realistic it is,

00:14:59.440 --> 00:15:01.340
I'm sorry, it's not doing much.

00:15:03.560 --> 00:15:05.620
Han: Don't do this, Luke.

00:15:05.620 --> 00:15:07.040
Come on, give me a sign here.

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:11.180
I know there's going to be a
bright "Star Wars" superfan

00:15:11.180 --> 00:15:12.240
that's going to come and tell me

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:13.520
exactly what animal that is.

00:15:14.840 --> 00:15:18.980
Han: But it'll keep you warm
till I get the shelter up.

00:15:20.060 --> 00:15:22.440
You should've had that
up at the first hint

00:15:22.440 --> 00:15:24.320
that the weather was going
to turn, had the shelter up.

00:15:24.320 --> 00:15:26.540
Secondly, in a survival situation,

00:15:26.540 --> 00:15:29.420
you take shelter where you can get it.

00:15:29.420 --> 00:15:31.880
I've heard of people
sheltering in animal carcasses

00:15:31.880 --> 00:15:33.300
in desert environments

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:35.240
where they've come across a dead animal,

00:15:35.240 --> 00:15:36.540
but I've never heard of anyone

00:15:36.540 --> 00:15:38.060
finding an animal big enough

00:15:38.060 --> 00:15:42.260
in an arctic environment to sleep inside.

00:15:42.260 --> 00:15:44.140
The average polar bear's probably about 8,

00:15:44.140 --> 00:15:46.340
8 1/2 feet tall, and
that's it standing up.

00:15:46.340 --> 00:15:48.840
To fit inside of a polar bear torso,

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:50.220
it's going to be a lot of work.

00:15:50.220 --> 00:15:52.900
[wind whooshing]

00:15:55.120 --> 00:15:57.580
If you're in the Arctic and
you need shelter quickly,

00:15:57.580 --> 00:16:00.860
your best bet is to
try and dig a snow hole

00:16:00.860 --> 00:16:03.460
or just pile up some snow

00:16:03.460 --> 00:16:05.500
to give you a break from the wind.

00:16:05.500 --> 00:16:09.300
Gosh, I'm a "Star Wars" fan.

00:16:09.300 --> 00:16:11.240
I'd give this a 5 out of 10.

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:14.220
[men grunting]

00:16:14.220 --> 00:16:15.720
Man: Hold the back!

00:16:19.500 --> 00:16:20.580
Hold it at the back!

00:16:20.580 --> 00:16:24.220
In Antarctica, the terrain is treacherous.

00:16:24.220 --> 00:16:28.380
Antarctica has the longest
mountain range in the world.

00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:30.920
It's got areas where it's ice rubble.

00:16:30.920 --> 00:16:33.540
You can see in this scene,
there were piles of ice,

00:16:33.540 --> 00:16:35.440
they were pushing this little long boat.

00:16:35.440 --> 00:16:38.780
That vision of seeing
completely flat arctic tundra

00:16:38.780 --> 00:16:41.660
or completely flat terrain, it's a myth.

00:16:41.660 --> 00:16:44.500
It very, very rarely presents itself.

00:16:44.500 --> 00:16:47.580
Man: I'm not doing this, it's stupid.

00:16:47.580 --> 00:16:48.840
Look at the hull.

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:50.540
You're supposed to be
captain of this cutter,

00:16:50.540 --> 00:16:52.560
and you're tearing it apart!

00:16:52.560 --> 00:16:54.820
Nowadays, I think people
tell you more about

00:16:54.820 --> 00:16:56.940
what you're doing and why you're doing it.

00:16:56.940 --> 00:16:58.080
I've been on expeditions

00:16:58.080 --> 00:17:00.340
where similar things have happened.

00:17:00.340 --> 00:17:02.040
We were walking across the Arctic,

00:17:02.040 --> 00:17:04.100
we're day 10, day 11 in.

00:17:04.100 --> 00:17:07.740
My pulk, which is my sled,
just started to feel heavier.

00:17:07.740 --> 00:17:10.400
And I realized that it was irrational

00:17:10.400 --> 00:17:12.980
when I went to my teammate,
they'd walked ahead,

00:17:12.980 --> 00:17:16.320
I said, "Look, my pulk's heavy.
You guys packed it wrong."

00:17:16.320 --> 00:17:17.440
My teammate laughed at me and said,

00:17:17.440 --> 00:17:20.240
"But, Dwayne, you packed
your pulk yourself."

00:17:20.240 --> 00:17:22.900
Fundamentally, I hadn't
eaten properly that morning

00:17:22.900 --> 00:17:24.480
and the evening before.

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:26.440
And that's all it took
for me to feel weaker,

00:17:26.440 --> 00:17:28.780
tired, drained, and fatigued.

00:17:28.780 --> 00:17:30.100
I'd give it a 6 or 7.

00:17:30.100 --> 00:17:31.420
I'd give it a 7 out of 10.

00:17:31.420 --> 00:17:34.260
So, my journey into exploration
was a bit of a strange one.

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:37.120
I googled North Pole.
A few names popped up,

00:17:37.120 --> 00:17:39.180
and one of the names was Matthew Henson.

00:17:39.180 --> 00:17:41.280
And I was drawn to Matthew Henson

00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:43.220
because he was a Black man.

00:17:43.220 --> 00:17:45.320
And I thought, "I've
never seen a Black man

00:17:45.320 --> 00:17:47.640
in any arctic book or anything."

00:17:47.640 --> 00:17:49.940
I think when people watch TVs and movies

00:17:49.940 --> 00:17:52.580
and shows and read, the
biggest myth they get

00:17:52.580 --> 00:17:54.940
is that only one type
of person goes there.

00:17:54.940 --> 00:17:56.720
You always see, it's a white male

00:17:56.720 --> 00:18:01.280
who's about 50 years old,
bearded, who's well weathered,

00:18:01.280 --> 00:18:03.200
got some money behind them that does this,

00:18:03.200 --> 00:18:04.300
and that's not the truth.

00:18:04.300 --> 00:18:06.040
We want more women doing it.

00:18:06.040 --> 00:18:08.140
We want more people from all
different backgrounds doing it.

00:18:08.140 --> 00:18:10.800
If we follow what we're seeing on TV

00:18:10.800 --> 00:18:13.300
and in magazines and historical accounts,

00:18:13.300 --> 00:18:17.540
you'd think that no women
had ever been to the pole.

00:18:17.540 --> 00:18:18.360
Thanks for watching.

00:18:18.360 --> 00:18:20.780
If you liked this, click
on the video above.

